fix(D-4): migration 012 halts chain on hosts without psql binary

Root cause (reproduced + independently verified against PG16 docs):
The pg-client path sends the whole migration file as ONE client.query() call.
node-pg ships multi-statement strings through the simple-query protocol, and
PostgreSQL wraps such a message in an implicit transaction block. Migration 012
defines PL/pgSQL procedures whose chunk-commit design requires internal COMMIT
(25M-row memory bounding), then CALLs them. PG docs (CALL): 'If CALL is executed
in a transaction block, then the called procedure cannot execute transaction
control statements.' Result: SQLSTATE 2D000 'invalid transaction termination',
the chain halts at 012 on any Windows/Node-only host lacking psql.

Reproduction (bidirectional, real PG 16.15 on this host):
  unfixed head: '[LEDGER FATAL] ... 012_partition_grades_attendance.sql:
                invalid transaction termination', DB atomically stays at 011
  fixed tree:   '[LEDGER] Applied 25 migration(s)', then 'Applied 0' (idempotent)

Minimal fix:
- splitSqlStatements(): a SQL-aware splitter (dollar-quotes incl. digit tags,
  E''/C'' backslash escapes, doubled quotes, double-quoted identifiers, line and
  block comments) splits only when needed.
- hasInternalTransactionControl(): trigger fires only on COMMIT + (PROCEDURE or
  CALL) — verified across all 25 files, only 012 matches.
- migrateUp: when psql is unavailable and the trigger fires, statements run one
  message at a time (each gets its own autocommit block), then the ledger row is
  written in its own transaction — mirroring the psql path's hasInternalTx mode.
- crash-window recovery: the ALREADY_APPLIED guard was gated on usePsql, so the
  same crash window was unrecoverable without psql. The gate is dropped and the
  recovery write now uses the client path too; a wipe of ledger 012+ with a
  post-025 schema now recovers to 25/25 with matching checksums (verified).

Independent review (delegated agent) drove two hardenings: digit-allowed
dollar-quote tags, double-quoted-identifier and E/C-string state in the splitter,
and the recovery-gate fix above. Residual: the split path is not whole-file
atomic by design; 012 is documented multi-phase with its own crash contract, and
the ledger stays authoritative with checksum verification.

Verified against the real PG16 instance on this host (fresh scratch DBs):
  setup -> 25 applied; positive -> partitioned roots + yearly partitions + row
  round-trip; failure -> 2D000 reproduction; recovery -> 25/25 with checksums;
  post-condition -> down-all leaves 0 tables. npm test 547/547 unchanged.
